---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Teaching — CMNA"
  description="Ways to use CMNA in a numerical-analysis course or computational methods sequence."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Teaching</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Use the code to expose the mathematics.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        CMNA works best when the implementation is not the destination. The code is
        a manipulable representation of the method: something students can inspect,
        break, compare, and improve.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell content">
      <h2>A useful course rhythm</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>State the numerical problem and the mathematical assumptions.</li>
        <li>Work through a method by hand far enough to make the update rule concrete.</li>
        <li>Read the implementation as an algorithm, not just as R syntax.</li>
        <li>Run a small example and inspect convergence or error.</li>
        <li>Change an assumption, tolerance, starting value, or data set and see what breaks.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Where CMNA fits</h2>
      <p>
        The book can serve as a collateral text in a traditional numerical-analysis
        course, or as the computational spine of a course that already supplies more
        theorem-and-proof treatment elsewhere. Its strongest role is the space
        between a derivation on the board and a production numerical library.
      </p>

      <h2>Materials that belong here</h2>
      <p>
        This section will collect course mappings, selected exercises, datasets,
        demonstrations, and instructor notes when they directly support the book.
        It will not try to become a complete numerical-analysis curriculum.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
